  "card": {
    "title": "Supergiant",
    "pillar": "science",
    "explanation": "Supergiants are among the most massive and most luminous stars. They stand out because their brightness and size push them to the top region of the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, a standard way astronomers organize stars. That high luminosity is the key feature that separates them from ordinary stars.\n\nIn practical terms, supergiants have absolute visual magnitudes roughly between −3 and −8, and temperatures from about 3,400 K up to over 20,000 K. This means some are relatively cool red supergiants near 3,400 K, while others are very hot blue supergiants above 20,000 K. Their extreme brightness makes them important beacons in studies of distant stellar populations.",
